Yes, this is entirely possible. Use a **dict with tuple keys**, not a NumPy array or list. Each `GameMap` already contains its own internal tile array; you just need a container mapping chunk coordinates to `GameMap` instances. Generate chunks lazily and cache them so player edits persist when returning:

```python
class World:
    def __init__(self, chunk_w, chunk_h):
        self.chunks = {}          # {(chunk_x, chunk_y): GameMap}
        self.chunk_w, self.chunk_h = chunk_w, chunk_h

    def get_chunk(self, cx, cy):
        if (cx, cy) not in self.chunks:
            self.chunks[(cx, cy)] = generate_chunk(cx, cy)  # returns a GameMap
        return self.chunks[(cx, cy)]
```

Track the player in *global* coordinates and derive the current chunk with floor division:

```python
cx, cy = player.x // self.chunk_w, player.y // self.chunk_h
```

When the player crosses a chunk boundary, swap `engine.game_map = world.get_chunk(cx, cy)` and offset entity positions by `-cx * chunk_w, -cy * chunk_h` so per-chunk rendering and FOV still work. Feed the chunk coordinates into seeded noise (`tcod.noise`) so generation is deterministic and neighboring chunks can match up.
